    "## Exercise 1 answers\n",
    "\n",
    "### Your earlier answers\n",
    "\n",
    "I could not recover your original answers from the available conversation history or notebook state. I have not attributed any answer to you or reconstructed your wording. Paste your original answers here to preserve them separately.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Copilot's answers\n",
    "\n",
    "**a)** The mean of the $k$ held-out errors estimates how the learning procedure performs on unseen observations when trained on approximately $n(k-1)/k$ observations; each fold uses a different fitted model, so this is not the error of the single model ultimately fitted on all $n$ observations.\n",
    "\n",
    "**b)** The estimate is $\\bar e=\\frac{1}{k}\\sum_{i=1}^{k}e_i$, with the usual reported standard error $s/\\sqrt{k}$, where $s$ is the sample standard deviation of the fold errors. The fold errors are dependent because their training sets overlap, so treating them as independent tends to make this standard error too small. As $k$ increases, each fold trains on a larger fraction of the data and the CV estimate generally has less training-size bias; its variance can increase, and leave-one-out CV is not guaranteed to have either zero bias or low variance.\n",
    "\n",
    "**c)** Fitting a scaler on all observations leaks validation-fold information—for example, validation observations contribute to the feature mean and standard deviation. Putting the scaler and estimator in a `Pipeline` makes cross-validation fit the scaler only on each fold's training split, then apply that fitted transform to both the training and validation portions.\n",
    "\n",
    "**d)** First, the learning rate was selected using the test set, so that set is no longer an independent final evaluation. Second, reporting the minimum of ten noisy test errors is optimistically biased by trying many candidates. The honest procedure is to choose the learning rate using training data and cross-validation, refit the selected procedure on all training data, and evaluate the untouched test set once; only then report that single test MSE (without claiming that it selected the learning rate).\n",
    "\n",
    "**e)** The number of epochs controls training and can change generalisation, so it is a hyperparameter. Choose it from the epoch with the smallest validation-fold error (or mean validation error across folds), without using the final test set. Training error is not a suitable selection criterion because it usually decreases as training continues and does not indicate performance on unseen data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**f)** Find the configuration with the smallest mean CV MSE, then keep every configuration whose mean CV MSE is no more than one standard error above that minimum. Choose the simplest among those candidates—here, the largest $\\lambda$—because the observed CV differences are too small to distinguish reliably and stronger regularisation reduces model complexity. If the grid is over architectures rather than $\\lambda$, \"simplest\" means fewer hidden units and/or fewer hidden layers."
   ]
