    "## Exercise 1: pencil and paper — what cross-validation estimates, and what can go wrong\n",
    "\n",
    "### a) What the $k$-fold estimate estimates\n",
    "\n",
    "**The answer.** The $k$-fold cross-validation error estimates the expected\n",
    "error, on data from the same distribution that was never seen in training,\n",
    "of the *procedure* \"this architecture with these hyperparameters, trained\n",
    "this way on $n(k-1)/k$ points\", averaged over which $n(k-1)/k$ points those\n",
    "are. It is an estimate of the generalisation error of the procedure, not of\n",
    "the one network you finally train, because the $k$ fold models are $k$\n",
    "*different* networks (same recipe, different training sets, different\n",
    "weights) that are evaluated, averaged and thrown away; the network you keep\n",
    "is a new fit on all $n$ points, which cross-validation never evaluated. The\n",
    "estimate is slightly pessimistic for that final network, since the fold\n",
    "models had fewer points ($80$ instead of $100$ here), and it only says\n",
    "anything about the final network because we assume the recipe behaves\n",
    "similarly on $80$ and on $100$ points.\n",
    "\n",
    "Exercise 2f shows the consequence: the test MSE of the final network lands\n",
    "*below* the cross-validated estimate, and that is not a contradiction.\n",
    "\n",
    "### b) The mean, the standard error, the overlap of the folds, and the choice of $k$\n",
    "\n",
    "**The answer.** With fold errors $e_1,\\dots,e_k$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\bar e=\\frac1k\\sum_{j=1}^k e_j,\\qquad s^2=\\frac{1}{k-1}\\sum_{j=1}^k(e_j-\\bar e)^2,\\qquad\n",
    "\\mathrm{SE}=\\frac{s}{\\sqrt k},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Eq. (3.93) of the notes. The $k$ numbers are not independent: training set\n",
    "$j$ is all the data except fold $j$, so two training sets $j\\ne j'$ share\n",
    "the $k-2$ folds that are in neither, a fraction $(k-2)/(k-1)$ of their\n",
    "samples — $3/4$ for $k=5$, $8/9$ for $k=10$, $(n-2)/(n-1)\\to1$ for\n",
    "leave-one-out. Positively correlated fold errors give the mean a *larger*\n",
    "variance than independent ones would, $\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar e)=\\frac{s^2}{k}+\\frac{k-1}{k}\\,\\overline{\\mathrm{Cov}}$,\n",
    "so $s/\\sqrt k$ is optimistic (too small). As $k$ grows from $2$ to $n$: the\n",
    "training sets grow from $n/2$ to $n-1$ points, so the upward *bias* of the\n",
    "estimate (fewer points than the final model) shrinks; but the training\n",
    "sets become more and more alike, the fold errors more and more correlated,\n",
    "and the *variance* of the estimate grows (and so does the cost: $n$ fits\n",
    "for leave-one-out). $k=5$ or $10$ is the usual compromise (Section 2.12).\n",
    "\n",
    "**The illustration.** The two claims about $k$ can be checked, because for\n",
    "a linear model the whole exercise costs microseconds. We take a degree-5\n",
    "polynomial fitted by least squares on the Runge data (a fixed procedure, no\n",
    "tuning), generate $200$ independent data sets of $100$ points from the same\n",
    "distribution, and for each compute the $k$-fold estimate for\n",
    "$k\\in\\{2,5,10,25,100\\}$ together with the standard error $s/\\sqrt k$ it\n",
    "claims. The *true* generalisation error of the procedure trained on all\n",
    "$100$ points is measured once on $20\\,000$ fresh points per data set. We\n",
    "then compare, over the $200$ data sets: the mean of the CV estimate with\n",
    "the mean true error (bias), the standard deviation of the CV estimate across\n",
    "data sets (its real variability) with the average claimed standard error\n",
    "(what one data set thinks its variability is), and the fraction of shared\n",
    "samples between two training sets."
