    "Read the table row by row. The bias (mean CV estimate minus the true\n",
    "error of the final model) is $+0.010$ for $k=2$ — fifty training points\n",
    "instead of a hundred, a third more error — and shrinks towards zero as $k$\n",
    "grows, as claimed; from $k=5$ on it is below $0.001$. The real standard\n",
    "deviation of the estimate across data sets is large for $k=2$ (a degree-5\n",
    "polynomial on fifty points occasionally extrapolates badly at the ends of\n",
    "the interval) and then *hardly changes* from $k=5$ to $k=n$, $0.0051$ to\n",
    "$0.0043$: the variability of a CV estimate is dominated by *which 100\n",
    "points we were given*, and no choice of $k$ removes that. The claimed\n",
    "standard error $s/\\sqrt k$ does not fall like $1/\\sqrt k$ either — it is\n",
    "$0.0042$ for every $k\\ge5$, because $s$, the spread of the fold errors,\n",
    "grows as the folds get smaller (a fold of one point has a very noisy\n",
    "error), and the two effects cancel. It sits below the real spread for every\n",
    "$k$, by twenty per cent at $k=5$; the hundred fold errors of leave-one-out\n",
    "share $98/99$ of their training data and are far from independent, but\n",
    "what that costs is that the *real* spread stops shrinking, not that the\n",
    "claimed one explodes. The lesson stands in a milder form than the\n",
    "pencil answer suggests: $s/\\sqrt k$ is a usable but optimistic number,\n",
    "good for comparing configurations *on the same folds*, where the\n",
    "correlation partly cancels, and not to be read as the uncertainty of the\n",
    "number itself; and $k$ does not buy precision, only less bias, at a cost\n",
    "of $k$ fits.\n",
    "\n",
    "### c) The scaler inside the fold, and what the `Pipeline` guarantees\n",
    "\n",
    "**The answer.** If the scaler is fitted on all $n$ points first, the mean\n",
    "and the standard deviation of the held-out fold's inputs — concrete\n",
    "numbers, say $\\bar x_{\\mathrm{fold}}$ — have entered the transformation\n",
    "that the training points are trained on; the held-out fold is then no\n",
    "longer data the model has never seen, and the validation error is\n",
    "contaminated by it. The same holds for the mean and variance of $y$ if the\n",
    "targets are scaled, and much more dramatically for anything that looks at\n",
    "$y$, such as a feature selection. A `Pipeline` guarantees the right order\n",
    "because `cross_validate` calls `pipeline.fit(X_train, y_train)` on the\n",
    "training part of each fold, which refits *every* step (the scaler included)\n",
    "on that part only, and then `pipeline.predict(X_val)`, which applies the\n",
    "stored training statistics to the held-out part.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The illustration.** For the Runge network the leak through the scaler is\n",
    "small — one input, a hundred points, folds of twenty — but it is there, and\n",
    "it is in the wrong direction. We run the untuned $1\\to50\\to1$ network of\n",
    "exercise 2 both ways on the same folds: scaler fitted on all 100 points\n",
    "(wrong) and on the training part of each fold (right, which is what\n",
    "`make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), net)` does). (For *ordinary least squares\n",
    "on polynomial features* the leak has no effect at all: rescaling $x$ by an\n",
    "affine map only reparametrises the polynomial, and the predictions are\n",
    "invariant — which is exactly why the point is easy to miss on Project 1\n",
    "and bites on Project 2, where the fit is not invariant.)"
