    "Six grid points move the network from $0.030$ to $0.023$ — below the\n",
    "degree-5 polynomial's $0.035$ and within a factor of two of the noise\n",
    "floor $\\sigma^2=0.01$; the rest of the way, with error bars, is\n",
    "exercise 2.\n",
    "\n",
    "That is all a cross-validation is: a permutation cut into $k$ pieces, a\n",
    "scaler fitted $k$ times, a model trained $k$ times from scratch, and $k$\n",
    "numbers summarised by their mean and standard error. `scikit-learn` adds\n",
    "the bookkeeping, the parallelism (`n_jobs`), the refit, and — not least —\n",
    "the guarantee that the scaler never sees the validation fold. Use the\n",
    "library in Project 2; keep this section for the day you need to know what\n",
    "it did."
=======
   "id": "aaa4e277",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The best of ten reports $0.0079$, on average, for a model whose true\n",
    "error is $0.0100$: a twenty-per-cent improvement that does not exist, and\n",
    "the colleague's $0.0094$ is well inside what selection alone produces. The\n",
    "same arithmetic applies to the *best CV score of a grid* (the last row of\n",
    "the self-check): the minimum over 25 grid points is biased downward in the\n",
    "same way, which is why the test set is used once, afterwards, on the\n",
    "single model the grid selected — exercise 2f.\n",
    "\n",
    "### e) Early stopping: the number of epochs is a hyperparameter\n",
    "\n",
    "**The answer.** The number of epochs changes the fitted function exactly as\n",
    "$\\lambda$ or the width does — a network trained for 100 epochs and the same\n",
    "network trained for 3000 are different models with different test errors —\n",
    "so it has to be chosen like any other hyperparameter, on data the model is\n",
    "not trained on. It cannot be chosen by the training error, which with a\n",
    "small enough learning rate is non-increasing in the number of epochs by\n",
    "construction: it would always say \"more\". The procedure is early stopping:\n",
    "hold out a validation fold, record its error after every epoch (or every\n",
    "few), take the epoch at which it is smallest, and train the final model for\n",
    "that many epochs (or keep the weights of that epoch). Averaged over the $k$\n",
    "folds this is a validation curve over epochs, and exercise 2d computes it\n",
    "both the expensive way (`validation_curve`, one training run per grid\n",
    "value) and the cheap way (one training run per fold, the validation error\n",
    "recorded after every epoch).\n",
    "\n",
    "### f) The one-standard-error rule\n",
    "\n",
    "**The answer.** With the CV error $\\mathrm{CV}(\\lambda)$ and its standard\n",
    "error $\\mathrm{SE}(\\lambda)$ on a grid, let $\\lambda_{\\min}$ be the\n",
    "minimiser; the rule picks\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\lambda_{1\\mathrm{SE}}=\\max\\bigl\\{\\lambda:\\ \\mathrm{CV}(\\lambda)\\le\\mathrm{CV}(\\lambda_{\\min})+\\mathrm{SE}(\\lambda_{\\min})\\bigr\\},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Eq. (3.96): the *most regularised* model whose CV error is within one\n",
    "standard error of the best. The models in that set cannot be told apart by\n",
    "the data — their differences are smaller than the noise of the estimate —\n",
    "so the apparent advantage of the less regularised one is not believed, and\n",
    "among indistinguishable models the simpler one is preferred: it has lower\n",
    "variance, it is less likely to be the beneficiary of the downward bias of\n",
    "part d), and (for the Lasso) it uses fewer features. When the grid is over\n",
    "hidden units, \"more regularised\" means *fewer* units — the smallest\n",
    "network within one standard error of the best; over epochs, the fewest\n",
    "epochs; over depth, the fewest layers. Exercise 2b applies the rule to the\n",
    "$(\\gamma,\\lambda)$ grid and exercise 3a to the width of the MNIST network."
