    "**Reading the grid.** Two features dominate the heat map, and both are\n",
    "about optimisation rather than about generalisation. The two smallest\n",
    "learning rates, $\\gamma=0.001$ and $0.003$, give a CV MSE of about $0.10$\n",
    "*for every* $\\lambda$ — that is the variance of $y$ (printed at the top,\n",
    "$0.10$): a thousand epochs of SGD at these rates have not moved the\n",
    "network away from predicting the mean, and the training MSE in panel (b)\n",
    "says the same. The column $\\lambda=10^{-2}$ gives $0.10$–$0.11$ for every\n",
    "$\\gamma$: with $n=100$ the penalty $\\lambda\\sum w^2$ is strong enough to\n",
    "pull all the weights to zero, and again the network predicts a constant.\n",
    "The remaining $3\\times4$ block is where the real comparison happens, and\n",
    "there the CV MSE falls monotonically with $\\gamma$: the minimum is at the\n",
    "largest learning rate on the grid, $\\gamma=0.1$, with $\\lambda=0$, at\n",
    "$0.0263\\pm0.0041$ — and because the minimum sits on the edge of the grid,\n",
    "the honest conclusion is that $\\gamma$ should be pushed further (or the\n",
    "number of epochs, which plays the same role: exercise 2d).\n",
    "\n",
    "**The one-standard-error rule.** Three cells are within one standard error\n",
    "of the minimum: $\\gamma=0.1$ with $\\lambda\\in\\{0,10^{-5},10^{-4}\\}$. The\n",
    "rule picks the most regularised of them, $\\gamma=0.1$, $\\lambda=10^{-4}$,\n",
    "with a CV MSE of $0.0303$: the data cannot distinguish a penalty of\n",
    "$10^{-4}$ from none, and the penalised model is preferred. This is the\n",
    "choice we carry to the rest of the exercise, and this table — the CV MSE\n",
    "with its standard error for every cell, the training MSE beside it, the\n",
    "minimum, the one-standard-error set and the chosen cell — is what Project\n",
    "2's heat map of part c) should show. The penalty itself does little on this\n",
    "problem, which is the right conclusion for a network that *underfits*:\n",
    "regularisation is the cure for the opposite disease."
   ]
  },
