    "**Width.** Along one hidden layer the CV MSE does not resolve a trend:\n",
    "the five values, $0.025$ to $0.030$, sit within about one standard error\n",
    "of each other (the smallest network, five units and sixteen parameters, is\n",
    "nominally the best), and the training MSE moves with them rather than\n",
    "falling away from them. The classical picture of Section 2.10 — training\n",
    "error falling, validation error turning up, the gap between them opening\n",
    "— is the picture of *overfitting*, and this network is not there: at\n",
    "$\\gamma=0.1$ and 1000 epochs of SGD the width of one layer is not the\n",
    "bottleneck. **Depth** is a different story, in both directions. The\n",
    "architectures Project 2 asks for *are* distinguishable: two hidden layers\n",
    "of 50 units give $0.0208\\pm0.0019$ and two of 100 give\n",
    "$0.0178\\pm0.0019$, against $0.030$ and $0.028$ for one layer of the same\n",
    "widths — three to four standard errors better, and the only cell within\n",
    "one standard error of the best is the best itself, so the\n",
    "one-standard-error rule picks $(100,100)$ as well. A second layer gives\n",
    "the network a composition of sigmoids that fits the peak of the Runge\n",
    "function far better than one layer of any width does with this\n",
    "optimiser. In the other direction, *three* hidden layers of 25 sigmoid\n",
    "units do not train at all: $0.108$, the variance of $y$, with the same\n",
    "number on the training data — every additional sigmoid layer shrinks the\n",
    "gradient that reaches the first one (Section 8.11, the vanishing\n",
    "gradient), and plain SGD at $\\gamma=0.1$ for 1000 epochs never gets the\n",
    "weights moving. That failure would be invisible in a report that only\n",
    "showed the two-layer result, and it is the reason the training MSE is\n",
    "tabulated next to the CV MSE: a network that underfits its *training*\n",
    "data has an optimisation problem, not a generalisation problem, and no\n",
    "amount of cross-validation over $\\lambda$ will fix it. The cost is in\n",
    "the last column: the two-layer networks have twenty to seventy times the\n",
    "parameters and two to three times the fitting time of the one-layer ones.\n",
    "We carry the $(100,100)$ network to the benchmark of e) alongside the\n",
    "one-layer choice of b).\n",
    "\n",
    "### d) Epochs, chosen by early stopping (optional), two ways\n",
    "\n",
    "**The expensive way** is `validation_curve` over `epochs`: every grid value\n",
    "retrains the network from scratch, $6\\times5=30$ runs of up to 2000\n",
    "epochs. **The cheap way** uses the fact that SGD passes through every epoch\n",
    "on the way to the last one: train each fold model *once* for 2000 epochs\n",
    "and record the validation MSE after every epoch. With the week-41 `train`\n",
    "this needs no new code — calling it 2000 times with `epochs=1` and the same\n",
    "`rng` and `layers` continues exactly where it stopped, so the trajectory\n",
    "is identical to one call with `epochs=2000`. The check at the end is that\n",
    "the two ways agree to every digit at the six grid values."
   ]
  },
