    "**Does the network beat the polynomial?** The one-hidden-layer network\n",
    "does not. The best polynomial — OLS of degree 11, twelve parameters, two\n",
    "milliseconds per fit — has a CV MSE of $0.0211\\pm0.0032$; Ridge at the\n",
    "same degree with a small penalty is the same number. The network chosen in\n",
    "b) has $0.0303\\pm0.0053$, the grid minimum $0.0263$, and even after 2000\n",
    "epochs it has not caught up ($0.0258$). The difference between the chosen\n",
    "network and the polynomial is about one and a half combined standard\n",
    "errors — barely resolvable, and in the polynomial's favour. The two-layer\n",
    "network of c), $0.0178\\pm0.0019$, gets *below* the polynomial, by about\n",
    "one combined standard error, which is to say: not resolvably — at ten\n",
    "thousand parameters against twelve and a thousand times the fitting time.\n",
    "This is what Section 8.14 and the \"What you should take away\" list of\n",
    "Project 2 warn about: a one-dimensional smooth function with a hundred\n",
    "noisy points is a problem that linear regression on a good basis solves as\n",
    "well as any network, faster and with more reliable error bars, and the\n",
    "network's advantage — a basis it learns itself — only pays when the input\n",
    "has many dimensions and no obvious basis, as on MNIST. Panel (b) shows the\n",
    "refitted models on the data: the degree-11 polynomial and the two-layer\n",
    "network follow the peak; the one-layer network has it too low and too\n",
    "wide, which is where its extra MSE comes from. The polynomial pays for\n",
    "its peak at the ends of the interval, where it oscillates — the Runge\n",
    "phenomenon the function is named for — while both networks stay flat\n",
    "there. The polynomial's own\n",
    "one-standard-error set is degrees 8–11, so the rule picks degree 8 — four\n",
    "degrees cannot be told apart on a hundred points — and above degree 11\n",
    "the CV error explodes while the training error keeps falling: that is the\n",
    "overfitting picture that the networks of c) and d) never reached.\n",
    "\n",
